[00:00] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: Creator standing in a forest, chapter list on screen.
AUDIO: 'Today I want to introduce you to Warmwind OS. With Warmwind, we are introducing the first fully autonomous digital workforce. Warmwind combines the forefront of AI research with a stunning new user interface. See, our goal was to'

[00:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'take this forefront of AI, a workforce and put it at the fingertips of everyday people. We want you to be able to automate all'

[00:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your already known applications. Windows, Linux, Android, web applications, whatever it is. And we don\'t want you to have the trouble'

[00:39] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: Creator walking away from the camera in a forest.
AUDIO: 'of all the setup with APIs, MCPs, custom tooling, whatever that is. And see, I feel you, I hear you, there\'s a lot of hype out there'

[00:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'when it comes to new world changing AI tools or the source of them. And I don\'t think this is going to be for everyone, but I think if it\'s for you, it will make'

[01:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you incredibly happy. We are going to do this in three parts, just like a well-written newspaper article. We will start with a hook to get your attention. That\'s already done.'

[01:16] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: Creator standing in a forest, looking up.
AUDIO: 'Number two, we will show you the product and lead you how we get there. And number three, we will go into even more details and show you how you can get your hands on it today. So why we were building Warmwind?'

[01:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'We looked at the space of AI and agents and working tools, automation. And what we saw is what we saw were a lot'

[01:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of chat interfaces, very text heavy, a lot of text, very fast, streaming down. And everything felt very dark and ugly, but also you had to be'

[01:55] [scene:explanation]
VISUAL: Creator in a forest, icons for ChatGPT, research, and chat on screen.
AUDIO: 'like a pro to use it. You had to connect APIs, MCPs. And if you weren\'t that technical or if you weren\'t a pro at that particular tool, you really had to get a tutorial to even'

[02:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'get the simplest tasks automated. See, we don\'t want to hate on chat interfaces. We love them. Chat GPT is great. We even use coding agents.'

[02:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And for these things, they are great for research, to chat, to brainstorm. But 90 percent of our work happens not in a text space. It happens in a visual space.'

[02:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This is today, and this will also be happening in the future because we humans are incredibly visual. And we thought if you take this core idea and if you combine it with German'

[02:46] [scene:analogy]
VISUAL: Creator demonstrating a Braun Citromatic juicer.
AUDIO: 'engineering and German industrial design, you don\'t optimize for features, but you optimize to have something simple and to get a core idea just right.'

[02:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'What would happen? Look, we have something for you. That\'s the brown, the dramatic. This particular machine we'

[03:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'have here was built 50 years ago and was designed by Dittorams in Germany. And even though it\'s almost or over 5 decades old, it still works like it\'s day one.'

[03:24] [scene:analogy]
VISUAL: Creator drinking orange juice from the Braun Citromatic.
AUDIO: 'Before I press down, I want you to admire this not even like an on off button. You just plug it in and then you do this. So simple, even a'

[03:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'kid, a child could do it. Let me plug it up. Great choose. And I want you to understand this even works what is minus'

[03:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'two degrees right here. So, but if you look at this, there\'s no update screen, there\'s no settings menu. There\'s not even a user menu. And that\'s not a lack of features.'

[04:07] [scene:explanation]
VISUAL: Creator standing in a forest, gesturing.
AUDIO: 'That is the feature. The core idea we wanted to take from that is to have something where you feel like you can\'t really do anything wrong.'

[04:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And these are two core principles we wanted to bring to AI. Number one, you take the most state of the art forefront of AI and bring it to'

[04:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'everyday people by number two, having user interface, which is a very important feature. It is incredibly simple, easy to use. And that\'s the most important part. You can\'t really do anything wrong. It has to feel like a kitchen appliance,'

[04:45] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Warmwind OS UI showing a scenic mountain background and a chat input.
AUDIO: 'just like we saw a second ago. And now, you know, there\'s a lot of talking, but let me show you what we\'ve built. (Music)'

[06:07] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Creator next to a laptop, Warmwind OS UI showing an app store with various app icons.
AUDIO: 'Now that the fancy part is done, allow me to have a jacket, it\'s freezing cold out here.'

[06:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, let me straight go into the product and show you how you can set up exactly the workflow you\'ve seen at the very beginning of the video. This is the user interface, as you just seen, and I\'ve already installed some apps.'

[06:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It\'s super easy to install them. You literally just go into the app store and get whatever you want. You have everything in there. Even if it\'s not in there, you can upload whatever you need. So, to set a task up, you'

[06:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can either use the chat input, and that\'s kind of okay, but it\'s also very complicated. You have to write a lot and explain a lot. What I like to use most of the time, and that\'s what I want to'

[06:57] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Creator next to a laptop, Warmwind OS UI showing Gmail open and a text input for instructions.
AUDIO: 'show you, is the teaching mode. And I think it\'s really magical. Just click Teach, and in this case, I will use the microphone version. In this version, I\'m literally just able to talk to the computer, and'

[07:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it\'s in real time listening to my voice and understanding, and mostly looking at what I\'m doing. So, now I will talk to Warmbint. So, this is the task I want you to do.'

[07:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Open up Gmail and work through all the emails. Open it up one by one, and inside the emails, you will have questions'

[07:35] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Creator next to a laptop, Warmwind OS UI showing SAP sales orders interface.
AUDIO: 'from different customers, and you should try to answer them with the help of SAP. SAP has everything you need to answer them most of the time.'

[07:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'If not, just hit me up. In this case, it\'s GNM bicycle. That\'s the company name, and there\'s an order number. So, we go over to SAP, go to Sold to Party, and enter the information there. Then we click Go and search it up,'

[07:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and whatever the information is, you will see it there and can just use it to answer the question. So, I click Finish. I didn\'t even like, I don\'t even need to go into the details.'

[08:11] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Creator next to a laptop, Warmwind OS UI showing SAP and a task progress overlay.
AUDIO: 'I just showed roughly what it has to do, and it will use this information and take a few seconds to compile it and work through it, and then it will come up with a work list. That\'s what you see right now at the edge of the screen.'

[08:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It slides in from the side and gives you a course overview of the general task it\'s planning to do. As we always say, we are not an assistant or a sidekick. We want to automate'

[08:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'long-term repetitive tasks. So, this is basically the whole track it always goes through every time it\'s starting its task, basically. So, the only thing that\'s really left to do for me here is to tell it when to do it, and I want it repetitive, obviously.'

[08:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, I tell it, do this every day or just all the time. And the thing is, one would know when it\'s missing information.'

[09:01] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Creator next to a laptop, Warmwind OS UI showing SAP and a "Your warmwind worker is currently running" message.
AUDIO: 'It will always just ask you for information it doesn\'t have, and in this case, it asks me when exactly to start. I don\'t really care, let\'s say 9 a.m. Now it says it\'s ready and scheduled every day, and you already see it starts moving, it\'s moving its cursor,'

[09:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'going through the first email, going back to SAP, going into the sole two part, and searching up the information it needs. And that\'s basically everything we need to do. At this point, we would'

[09:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'recommend to maybe watch over it for a few minutes or hours just to see how it goes. If it needs any information, it will hit you up. You can also always add information along the way. You can always interrupt it.'

[09:40] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Creator next to a laptop, Warmwind OS UI showing Gmail and a task progress sidebar.
AUDIO: 'And you have a small progress bar here on the side, which shows you the learning it\'s going through. If this reaches 100%, you can be sure that the AI will from there on just work'

[09:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'without you needing to be there. So it needs a little bit of time to figure things out, to get into the groove, and over time, it will learn to become better at the task, and also become much faster at the task.'

[10:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And this is basically the moment where I can literally take my laptop, close it, and we are ready to go, like time for holidays or something. One more thing, what I really need to bring across here'

[10:18] [scene:explanation]
VISUAL: Creator standing next to a laptop, gesturing.
AUDIO: 'is that even though we call it operating system, it\'s not replacing your operating system. Let me show you, like, this is literally what I showed you just from. You will be able to download'

[10:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the app for Mac, for Windows, or you can open it wherever you want, just in your browser. You really have to think about this of AI owning its own computer with its own applications. If you think about it,'

[10:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it really makes sense, because if you give it its task, which is super repetitive, and you spawn out a first one, but then a second one, a third one, a 10th one, a 100th one, and they all work along each other and do every day their chores, whatever,'

[10:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'something you don\'t really want to care about anymore. You don\'t want them to rely on your machine, on your time, on your input. They have to have their own computer in the cloud'

[11:06] [scene:security]
VISUAL: Animated graphic showing a server connected to multiple screens with shield icons.
AUDIO: 'and work even while your laptop is offline if you\'re at holiday or asleep. If we talk about AI having its own computer, security is incredibly important,'

[11:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and this is why we build War'

[11:46] [scene:outro]
VISUAL: Creator standing in front of a lake and mountains, next to a laptop.
AUDIO: 'companies on Earth with the high security standards. As we were building Warwind, there were three major things we had to get right. With current AI, it\'s most language models, and language models'

[11:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'have three big problems. The biggest problem is that they don\'t really understand vision as you and me. They can see things, they can even create images, but they don\'t live in the visual world. They live in a text'

[12:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'world and also sometimes understand images a little bit, which means if you give a user interfaces, if you give it everyday real work, they\'re catastrophically failing and we didn\'t get them to work. The second thing is'

[12:27] [scene:outro]
VISUAL: Creator standing in front of a lake and mountains, next to a laptop.
AUDIO: 'they\'re incredibly slow. If you use current state of the art language models, they would use up to 30 or even 60 seconds for a single click, and this click wouldn\'t re-click, it would more be a description of a click, and then some AI'

[12:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'model would have to do it. But the third thing, and that\'s the most problematic one, is pricing. They\'re incredibly expensive. If you take current state of the art language models and try to do something like whirlwind, you pay 1,000 or even 2,000'

[12:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'euros or dollars per hour, and given the current speed, at this price, it\'s completely unfeasible, which made us realize that language models are not the way to go if you want to have a product like that.'

[13:06] [scene:outro]
VISUAL: Creator standing in front of a lake and mountains, next to a laptop, gesturing.
AUDIO: 'What we had to do is to take a lot of inspiration from robotics and embodied AI and rethink how AI could work in their user interface. So what we developed is an incredibly intuitive, super efficient, and yet powerful AI,'

[13:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'which is optimized to run repetitive, real-world tasks for you every day. Starting now, you can get immediate access to whirlwind for 24 euros a week. With that, you can have multiple workers,'

[13:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and there will even be a free version, but it will be very limited. Overall, we\'ve made capacity for 10,000 users. Everything for me now left to say is to wish you a lot of fun exploring the product, bring it into your company,'

[13:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and see which kind of repetitive workflows it can do for you.'
